WN.lb(12,{"mt_ChfDeaNk5a":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"f′ is positive for x < 2 and negative for x > 2. What does f have at x = 2?","choices":["A maximum","A minimum","A point where it crosses the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Positive gradient means going up. What comes after up then down?","explain":"Up, then flat, then down is a hill, so f has a maximum at x = 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"f has turning points at x = {a} and x = {b} and no others. Add the x-values where f′ = 0.","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[-5,-1],"b":[1,5]},"hint":"f′ = 0 exactly at the turning points of f.","explain":"The zeros of f′ are x = {a} and x = {b}, and {a} + {b} = {=a+b}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"f(x) = x². Which describes the graph of f′?","choices":["A straight line through the origin","A U-shape, like f","Always above the x-axis, since x² ≥ 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"For x < 0, is the curve y = x² going up or down?","explain":"x² goes down for x < 0 and up for x > 0, with a flat point at 0. So f′ is negative, then 0, then positive: the line y = 2x."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where f is going down most steeply, what is f′ doing?","choices":["Reaching its lowest (most negative) value","Crossing the x-axis","Reaching its highest value"],"answer":0,"hint":"Steep and downhill: is the gradient a big positive or a big negative number?","explain":"The steepest downhill point has the most negative gradient, so f′ is at a minimum there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"The tangent to y = f(x) at x = 1 passes through (1, {a}) and (3, {b}). What is f′(1)?","answer":"(b-a)/2","params":{"a":[0,6],"b":[7,15]},"hint":"Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x, and here x changes by 2.","explain":"Gradient = ({b} − {a}) ÷ (3 − 1) = {=(b-a)/2}, so f′(1) = {=(b-a)/2}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A student sketches f′ by copying the graph of f and moving it down. What went wrong?","choices":["f′ shows the slope of f, not its shape or height","Nothing; that is the method","They should have moved it up instead"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does each value of f′ measure?","explain":"f′(x) is the gradient of f at x. A copy of f shows heights, so its zeros and signs come out in the wrong places."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"f is a W shape with three turning points. How many times does the graph of f′ meet the x-axis?","choices":["3","2","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each flat point of f gives one zero of f′.","explain":"Three turning points give three zeros of f′. For f(x) = x⁴/4 − x², f′(x) = x³ − 2x has zeros at −√2, 0 and √2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"On one stretch, f is below the x-axis and going up. What is the sign of f′ there?","choices":["Positive","Negative","Zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sign of f′ comes from the slope of f, not from where f is.","explain":"f is climbing, so its gradient is positive. Being below the axis does not change that."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"The graph of f′ is a U-shaped parabola crossing the x-axis at x = −1 and x = 1. Which best describes f?","choices":["A cubic with a maximum at x = −1 and a minimum at x = 1","A cubic with a minimum at x = −1 and a maximum at x = 1","A U-shaped parabola with its minimum at x = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the sign of f′ in each part: left of −1, between, right of 1.","explain":"f′ is +, then −, then +, so f goes up, down, up: a maximum at x = −1 and a minimum at x = 1."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On the graph of f, where are the zeros of f′?","choices":["At the x-values of the turning points of f","Where f crosses the x-axis","At the heights of the turning points"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the tangent to f flat?","explain":"A flat tangent has gradient 0, and that happens at the turning points of f. Use their x-values."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The tangent to y = f(x) at x = 2 passes through (2, {a}) and (3, {b}). What is f′(2)?","answer":"b-a","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[6,12]},"hint":"f′(2) is the gradient of this tangent: change in y over change in x.","explain":"Gradient = ({b} − {a}) ÷ (3 − 2) = {=b-a}, so f′(2) = {=b-a}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Just after a maximum, f is above the x-axis and going down. What is the sign of f′ there?","choices":["Negative","Positive","Zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the slope, not at the height of f.","explain":"f is going down, so its tangent slopes down and f′ is negative, even though f itself is above the axis."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A curve y = f(x) has a minimum at x = {a}. What is f′({a})?","answer":"0*a","params":{"a":[-6,6]},"hint":"At a minimum, what does the tangent look like?","explain":"The tangent at a minimum is flat, so its gradient is 0: f′({a}) = {=0*a}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"f is a quadratic with a maximum, like an upside-down U. What does the graph of f′ look like?","choices":["A straight line sloping down","A U-shaped parabola","A flat line on the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"f goes up, is flat once, then goes down. What does f′ do?","explain":"f′ is positive, then 0 at the maximum, then negative. For a quadratic it is a straight line, so it slopes down through the axis."}],"explain":{"kid":["Pick a point on a curve and draw its tangent. The tangent's gradient tells you how steep the curve is there. Do this at every x, and plot each gradient as a dot. The dots make a new graph. That graph is f′(x), the gradient function.","Start with the flat points. At the top of a hill or the bottom of a valley, the tangent is flat. Its gradient is 0. So at that same x, the graph of f′ sits on the x-axis. Use the x of the turning point, not its height.","Next, the signs. Where f goes uphill, left to right, its gradient is positive, so f′ is above the axis. Where f goes downhill, f′ is below the axis. It does not matter if f itself is above or below the axis. Only the slope counts.","Last, the size. A steep part of f gives a value of f′ far from the axis. A gentle part gives a value close to it. So a cubic with a hill and a valley has an f′ shaped like a U that dips below the axis between the two turning points."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, asks students to sketch the graph of f′ from the graph of f without a formula. It rests on one idea: f′(a) is the gradient of the tangent to y = f(x) at x = a. Reading that gradient across the whole graph produces the gradient function.","A reliable method has three passes. First, mark the x-values of the turning points of f on the new axes as zeros of f′. Second, decide the sign between them: f′ > 0 where f is increasing and f′ < 0 where it is decreasing. Third, judge size: steep sections give large |f′|, and the steepest point of a section is a maximum or minimum of f′.","Shapes follow from this. A quadratic gives a straight line, a cubic with two turning points gives a parabola with two roots, and a quartic W gives a cubic with three roots. For a polynomial, the degree drops by one. The sine curve gives a cosine-shaped curve.","Common mistakes: plotting the zeros of f′ at the heights of the turning points; giving f′ the sign of f rather than of its slope; copying the shape of f and shifting it; and putting a zero of f′ where f crosses the x-axis. A quick check is to pick one point, draw its tangent and see if the sign and rough size agree."]},"familyTip":"Draw a wiggly line on paper: a hill, then a valley. Ask your teenager to walk a pencil along it and say 'up', 'flat' or 'down' at each part. Then ask them to draw a second graph underneath that is above the line for 'up', on it for 'flat' and below it for 'down'.","keyIdea":"The graph of f′(x) shows the gradient of f at each x. Where f is flat (a turning point), f′ = 0, so each turning point of f becomes a point where f′ meets the x-axis. Where f goes up, f′ is above the axis; where f goes down, f′ is below it. Steeper means further from the axis. f′ copies the slope of f, never its height.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each part of a graph of f by what it tells you about f′ there.","opts":{"bins":["f′ > 0","f′ = 0","f′ < 0"],"items":[["f climbs from left to right",0],["f is below the axis but rising",0],["the top of a hill on f",1],["the bottom of a valley on f",1],["f falls from left to right",2],["f is above the axis but falling",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggradf","caption":"Flagship lab: drag along f and drop each tangent gradient onto the f′ axes, mark zeros and signs, sketch f′ with handles, then match graphs.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_CyACN8L0PA":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does \"momentarily at rest\" mean?","choices":["v = 0","s = 0","a = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rest is about motion, not position.","explain":"At rest means the velocity is zero, v = 0. s = 0 means it is back at the start, which could happen while it is moving."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"s = {a}t³ + {b}t. Find the velocity at t = 2.","answer":"12*a+b","hint":"v = ds/dt = 3({a})t² + {b}; put t = 2.","explain":"v = {=3*a}t² + {b}, so at t = 2, v = {=12*a} + {b} = {=12*a+b} m/s.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"s = t³ − {=3*k}t² + 5t. At what time is the acceleration zero?","answer":"k","hint":"Differentiate twice, then solve a = 0.","explain":"v = 3t² − {=6*k}t + 5 and a = 6t − {=6*k}. a = 0 when t = {k} s.","params":{"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"s = t³ − 6t² + 9t. At t = 1 the particle is at rest. What is its acceleration?","choices":["−6 m/s²","0 m/s²","9 m/s²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a = 6t − 12 and substitute t = 1.","explain":"a = 6(1) − 12 = −6 m/s². Being at rest does not mean the acceleration is zero."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"At t = 2, s = 2 m and v = −3 m/s. Which way is the particle moving?","choices":["In the negative direction","In the positive direction","It is not moving"],"answer":0,"hint":"Direction comes from v, not s.","explain":"v is negative, so it moves in the negative direction, even though it is still on the positive side of the start."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"v = 3t² − 12t + 9 and a = 6t − 12. On 2 < t < 3, is the particle speeding up?","choices":["No, v < 0 and a > 0, so it slows down","Yes, a > 0 so it speeds up","Yes, v < 0 so it speeds up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the sign of v and the sign of a on the interval.","explain":"For 2 < t < 3, v < 0 and a > 0. Opposite signs mean the acceleration acts against the motion, so it slows down."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"s = {=k}t² − t³. Find the velocity at t = 1.","answer":"2*k-3","hint":"v = ds/dt = 2({k})t − 3t².","explain":"v = {=2*k}t − 3t², so at t = 1, v = {=2*k} − 3 = {=2*k-3} m/s.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is the acceleration if s = 4t³ − t²?","choices":["24t − 2","12t² − 2t","12t − 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Differentiate twice: first s to v, then v to a.","explain":"v = 12t² − 2t, then a = dv/dt = 24t − 2. 12t² − 2t is only the velocity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"s = t³ − 6t² + 9t. When is the particle at rest?","choices":["t = 1 and t = 3","t = 0 and t = 3","t = 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Solve v = 3t² − 12t + 9 = 0.","explain":"3(t − 1)(t − 3) = 0 gives t = 1 and t = 3. t = 0 and t = 3 are where s = 0, and t = 2 is where a = 0."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"s = t³ − 6t² + 9t. Which is the velocity v?","choices":["3t² − 12t + 9","t³ − 6t² + 9","6t − 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Velocity is ds/dt: differentiate each term once.","explain":"v = ds/dt = 3t² − 12t + 9. 6t − 12 is the acceleration, a second derivative."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"s = {a}t² + {b}t (metres, t in seconds). Find the velocity at t = {c}.","answer":"2*a*c+b","hint":"Differentiate: v = 2({a})t + {b}, then put t = {c}.","explain":"v = {=2*a}t + {b}, so at t = {c}, v = {=2*a*c} + {b} = {=2*a*c+b} m/s.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,6],"c":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"v = {a}t² − {b}t. Find the acceleration at t = {c}.","answer":"2*a*c-b","hint":"Acceleration is dv/dt. Differentiate v, then substitute.","explain":"a = dv/dt = {=2*a}t − {b}, so at t = {c}, a = {=2*a*c} − {b} = {=2*a*c-b} m/s².","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3],"c":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"At some time, v = −4 m/s and a = −3 m/s². What is happening?","choices":["It is speeding up","It is slowing down","It is at rest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the signs of v and a, not a alone.","explain":"v and a are both negative, so the acceleration is in the direction of motion and the particle speeds up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"v = t² − {=p+q}t + {=p*q}. Find the first time (t > 0) the particle is at rest.","answer":"p","hint":"At rest means v = 0. Factorise the quadratic.","explain":"v = (t − {p})(t − {q}) = 0 gives t = {p} or t = {q}, so the first time is t = {p} s.","params":{"p":[1,3],"q":[4,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A particle moves along a line. Its displacement s is how far it is from the start, with a sign for the side. If s = t³ − 6t² + 9t, then v = ds/dt = 3t² − 12t + 9. Velocity is just the gradient of the s graph.","Differentiate again to get acceleration: a = dv/dt = 6t − 12. So a is the gradient of the v graph, and the second derivative of s.","The particle is at rest for an instant when v = 0. Here 3(t − 1)(t − 3) = 0, so t = 1 and t = 3. Do not solve s = 0 by mistake. At t = 0, s = 0 but v = 9, so it is moving.","The sign of v tells you the direction. At t = 2, s = 2 is positive but v = −3, so it moves back. To tell if it speeds up, compare signs. Same signs of v and a: faster. Opposite signs: slower."],"adult":["This A-level topic (ages 16 to 18) applies differentiation to motion in a straight line. Displacement s is a function of time, velocity is v = ds/dt and acceleration is a = dv/dt = d²s/dt². Every question here follows one chain: differentiate to move from s to v to a, and substitute or solve to answer it.","Three readings matter. First, momentarily at rest means v = 0, found by solving v(t) = 0. Second, the direction of motion is the sign of v; the sign of s only says which side of the start the particle is on. Third, speed increases when v and a have the same sign and falls when they have opposite signs, because then the acceleration acts against the motion.","Common mistakes: solving s = 0 instead of v = 0 for rest; assuming that at rest the acceleration is zero too (at t = 1 here, a = −6); reading a negative a as slowing down whatever the sign of v; differentiating only once when acceleration is asked for; and confusing distance travelled with displacement when the particle turns back. Be careful to state units: m, m/s and m/s².","In the lab learners build v = ds/dt and a = dv/dt term by term and drag a time cursor along linked s, v and a graphs while the particle moves on a track. They find a time when s is positive but v is negative, pin both rest times, pin where a = 0, and classify each interval as speeding up or slowing down."]},"keyIdea":"For motion in a straight line, velocity is the rate of change of displacement and acceleration is the rate of change of velocity: v = ds/dt and a = dv/dt = d²s/dt². The particle is momentarily at rest when v = 0. The sign of v gives the direction, not the sign of s. It speeds up when v and a have the same sign and slows down when they have opposite signs.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the particle speeding up or slowing down? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Speeding up","Slowing down"],"items":[["v = 5, a = 2",0],["v = −4, a = −3",0],["v = −1, a = −6",0],["v = 6, a = −2",1],["v = −3, a = 4",1],["v = 2, a = −1",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkin","caption":"Flagship lab: build v and a by differentiating, drag time along linked s, v, a graphs, find direction, rest times and when it speeds up.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_d4wGJHnGW3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Differentiate y = x³ ln x.","choices":["3x² ln x + x²","3x","3x² ln x"],"answer":0,"hint":"u = x³, v = ln x, and d(ln x)/dx = 1/x.","explain":"x³·(1/x) + ln x·3x² = x² + 3x² ln x. 3x is u′v′ and 3x² ln x drops the u v′ term."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"y = {a}x·x². Using the product rule with u = {a}x and v = x², find dy/dx at x = 2.","answer":"12*a","hint":"u′ = {a} and v′ = 2x. Then u v′ + v u′ = {a}x·2x + x²·{a}.","explain":"dy/dx = {=2*a}x² + {a}x² = {=3*a}x². At x = 2 that is {=3*a} × 4 = {=12*a}. Check: y = {a}x³ gives the same.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A student writes d(x² eˣ)/dx = 2x eˣ. What went wrong?","choices":["They multiplied the derivatives, u′v′","Nothing, it is correct","They should have added u and v"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare their answer with u v′ + v u′.","explain":"2x eˣ is u′ × v′. The full rule gives x²eˣ + 2x eˣ = x eˣ(x + 2)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Differentiate y = x² cos x.","choices":["2x cos x − x² sin x","2x cos x + x² sin x","−2x sin x"],"answer":0,"hint":"d(cos x)/dx = −sin x. Watch the sign.","explain":"u v′ + v u′ = x²(−sin x) + cos x·2x = 2x cos x − x² sin x. The plus sign forgets that cos x differentiates to −sin x."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"y = (x + {a})(x + {b}). Find dy/dx at x = 1.","answer":"2+a+b","hint":"Both factors have derivative 1, so dy/dx = (x + {a}) + (x + {b}).","explain":"dy/dx = 2x + {a} + {b}. At x = 1 that is 2 + {a} + {b} = {=2+a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"For y = x² eˣ, where is dy/dx = 0?","choices":["x = 0 and x = −2","x = 0 only","Nowhere, eˣ is never 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Factorise dy/dx = x²eˣ + 2x eˣ first.","explain":"dy/dx = x eˣ(x + 2). eˣ is never 0, so x = 0 or x = −2. Not factorising makes it easy to miss x = −2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"y = x³ ln x has dy/dx = x²(3 ln x + 1). For x > 0, where is dy/dx = 0?","choices":["x = e^(−1/3)","x = −1/3","x = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"x² is not 0 for x > 0, so solve 3 ln x + 1 = 0 and undo ln with e.","explain":"ln x = −1/3, so x = e^(−1/3), about 0.72. x = 0 is outside the domain of ln x."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"y = x²(x + {a}). Find dy/dx at x = {b}.","answer":"3*b*b+2*a*b","hint":"u = x², v = x + {a}. Then dy/dx = u v′ + v u′ = x²·1 + (x + {a})·2x.","explain":"dy/dx = x² + 2x(x + {a}) = 3x² + {=2*a}x. At x = {b}: {=3*b*b} + {=2*a*b} = {=3*b*b+2*a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Differentiate y = x sin x.","choices":["sin x + x cos x","cos x","x cos x"],"answer":0,"hint":"u = x, v = sin x. Write u′ and v′ first, then u v′ + v u′.","explain":"u v′ + v u′ = x cos x + sin x · 1 = sin x + x cos x. cos x alone is u′v′, the classic slip."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"y = (x + {a})(x − {b}). Find dy/dx at x = 0.","answer":"a-b","hint":"u = x + {a}, v = x − {b}, and both u′ and v′ are 1.","explain":"dy/dx = (x + {a})·1 + (x − {b})·1 = 2x + {a} − {b}. At x = 0 this is {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Differentiate y = x eˣ and factorise.","choices":["eˣ(x + 1)","eˣ","x eˣ⁻¹"],"answer":0,"hint":"d(eˣ)/dx = eˣ. Use u v′ + v u′, then take out eˣ.","explain":"x·eˣ + eˣ·1 = eˣ(x + 1). Just eˣ is u′v′, and x eˣ⁻¹ wrongly uses the power rule on eˣ."}],"explain":{"kid":["Say y is two things multiplied, like x² times eˣ. Call them u and v. To find the slope, keep u and differentiate v. Then keep v and differentiate u. Add the two. That is the product rule: u v′ + v u′.","Why two parts? Think of a box u wide and v tall. Its area is uv. If both sides grow a bit, the new area is a strip along the top and a strip down the side. There is a tiny corner too. As the step gets small, the corner gets so small it does not count.","Do not just multiply the two slopes. For x² times x³, which is x⁵, the true slope is 5x⁴. The slopes multiplied give 2x times 3x², which is 6x³. That is wrong. The rule gives 3x⁴ plus 2x⁴, which is 5x⁴. Right!","Tidy up by taking out what both parts share. For x² eˣ, the slope is x² eˣ plus 2x eˣ, which is x eˣ times (x + 2). Now you can see the slope is zero at x = 0 and at x = −2."],"adult":["The product rule states that if y = u(x)v(x), then dy/dx = u dv/dx + v du/dx. It follows from first principles. The change in uv is uΔv + vΔu + ΔuΔv. Divide by h and let h tend to zero. The last term vanishes, since Δu tends to zero while Δv/h tends to dv/dx.","Students meet it with standard results: the derivatives of xⁿ, eˣ, ln x, sin x and cos x. Typical tasks are x²eˣ, x sin x and x³ ln x. They are expected to simplify by taking out a common factor. That form makes stationary points easy to find: x eˣ(x + 2) = 0 gives x = 0 or x = −2.","The most common mistake is to multiply the derivatives, writing u′v′. Others are dropping one of the two terms, using the power rule on eˣ, sign slips with cos x, and solving ln x = −1/3 as x = −1/3. A good habit is to write u, v, u′ and v′ out in a list before you combine them.","The lab builds the rule from the growing rectangle. It tests u′v′ against a product with a known answer. It then builds and factorises two derivatives, and finds a flat tangent on y = x sin x."]},"keyIdea":"To differentiate a product y = uv, use d(uv)/dx = u dv/dx + v du/dx: keep one factor, differentiate the other, then swap and add. It is not du/dx × dv/dx. Picture a u-by-v rectangle growing: the new area is two strips, u·Δv and v·Δu, plus a corner that vanishes as the step shrinks. Factorise the answer to find where dy/dx = 0.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each line a correct use of the product rule? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Correct","❌ Slip"],"items":[["d(x sin x)/dx = sin x + x cos x",0],["d(x eˣ)/dx = eˣ(x + 1)",0],["d(x² ln x)/dx = 2x ln x + x",0],["d(x³ cos x)/dx = 3x² cos x − x³ sin x",0],["d(x sin x)/dx = cos x",1],["d(x² eˣ)/dx = 2x eˣ",1],["d(x ln x)/dx = ln x",1],["d(eˣ cos x)/dx = eˣ(cos x + sin x)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprod","caption":"Flagship lab: grow a u-by-v rectangle, test u′v′ against x⁵, build and factorise d(x²eˣ)/dx, find a flat tangent on x sin x, and solve for x³ ln x.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2zRjalBYAk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the linearisation of f(x) = √x at x = 9?","choices":["L(x) = 3 + (x − 9)/6","L(x) = 3 + (x − 9)/3","L(x) = 9 + (x − 9)/6"],"answer":0,"hint":"L(x) = f(a) + f′(a)(x − a), with f(9) = 3 and f′(x) = 1/(2√x).","explain":"f(9) = 3 and f′(9) = 1/(2 × 3) = 1/6, so L(x) = 3 + (x − 9)/6. Using 1/3 forgets the 2, and starting from 9 uses x instead of f(9)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"y = x². Estimate δy when x goes from {a} to {a} + {b}/10.","answer":"a*b/5","hint":"dy/dx = 2x. Evaluate it at x = {a} and multiply by δx = {b}/10.","explain":"At x = {a}, dy/dx = {=2*a}. δy ≈ {=2*a} × {=b/10} = {=a*b/5}.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"y = x⁴. Estimate the change in y when x increases from 1 to 1 + {b}/100.","answer":"4*b/100","hint":"dy/dx = 4x³, which is 4 at x = 1. Then use δy ≈ (dy/dx)δx.","explain":"dy/dx = 4 at x = 1 and δx = {=b/100}, so δy ≈ 4 × {=b/100} = {=4*b/100}.","params":{"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You estimate e^0.2 with the tangent to y = eˣ at x = 0. The estimate is:","choices":["too small, because eˣ is convex","too big, because eˣ is concave","exact, because the slope of eˣ is eˣ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the sign of the second derivative of eˣ.","explain":"f″(x) = eˣ > 0, so the curve bends up and lies above its tangent. The estimate 1.2 is below the true 1.2214."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Each side of a cube is increased by 1%. About how much does its volume increase?","choices":["3%","1%","0.03%"],"answer":0,"hint":"For V = x³, write δV/V in terms of δx/x.","explain":"δV ≈ 3x²δx, so δV/V ≈ 3δx/x = 3 × 1% = 3%. The exact rise is 1.01³ − 1 = 3.03%."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Use the tangent to y = ln x at x = 1 to estimate ln 1.1.","choices":["0.1","1.1","0.01"],"answer":0,"hint":"ln 1 = 0 and the slope of ln x is 1/x, which is 1 at x = 1.","explain":"ln 1.1 ≈ 0 + 1 × 0.1 = 0.1. The true value 0.0953 is a little lower, because ln x is concave. 1.1 forgets that ln 1 = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You halve δx in a tangent-line estimate. About what happens to the error?","choices":["It falls to about a quarter","It halves","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The error is about ½f″(a)δx². What happens to δx² when δx halves?","explain":"The error is roughly ½f″(a)δx², and (δx/2)² = δx²/4, so the error falls to about a quarter, not a half."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"y = x². Use δy ≈ (dy/dx)δx to estimate the change in y when x goes from {a} to {a} + {b}/100.","answer":"2*a*b/100","hint":"dy/dx = 2x, so at x = {a} the slope is {=2*a}. Multiply it by δx = {b}/100.","explain":"dy/dx = 2x = {=2*a} at x = {a}, and δx = {=b/100}. So δy ≈ {=2*a} × {=b/100} = {=2*a*b/100}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"y = x³. Estimate δy when x increases from {a} to {a}.1.","answer":"0.3*a*a","hint":"dy/dx = 3x². Work it out at x = {a}, then multiply by δx = 0.1.","explain":"dy/dx = 3x² = {=3*a*a} at x = {a}. So δy ≈ {=3*a*a} × 0.1 = {=0.3*a*a}.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Use the tangent to y = √x at x = 100 to estimate √101. Give 2 decimal places.","answer":10.05,"hint":"f(100) = 10 and f′(x) = 1/(2√x), so f′(100) = 1/20. The step is δx = 1.","explain":"√101 ≈ f(100) + f′(100)·δx = 10 + (1/20)(1) = 10.05. The true value is 10.0499, a touch lower, because √x is concave."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The radius of a sphere goes up by 2%. Estimate the percentage increase in its volume.","answer":6,"hint":"V = (4/3)πr³, so dV/dr = 4πr². Divide δV ≈ 4πr²δr by V.","explain":"δV/V ≈ 4πr²δr ÷ (4/3)πr³ = 3δr/r = 3 × 2% = 6%. The exact rise is 1.02³ − 1 = 6.12%."}],"explain":{"kid":["Zoom in on a smooth curve and it starts to look straight. Right next to the point, the curve and its tangent line are almost the same. So we can use the tangent in place of the curve for a short step.","Here is the recipe. Start at a point a where you know f(a) and the slope f′(a). Take a small step δx. The new value is about f(a) + f′(a) times δx. This is the linear approximation, also called the linearisation of f at a.","Try √4.1. You know √4 = 2. The slope of √x is 1/(2√x), so at 4 it is 1/4. The step is 0.1. So √4.1 is about 2 + 0.25 × 0.1 = 2.025. The true value is 2.02485, so we are very close.","Is it too big or too small? Look at the bend. √x bends down, so the tangent sits above it and the guess is a bit too big. eˣ bends up, so its tangent guess is a bit too small. Halve the step and the error drops to about a quarter. The same idea works for real things. A circle of radius 5 cm grows by 0.1 cm. The area changes by about 2πr times δr, which is π, or 3.14 cm². The exact change is 3.17 cm²."],"adult":["If f is differentiable at a, the tangent line L(x) = f(a) + f′(a)(x − a) is the linearisation of f at a. For a small change δx this gives f(a + δx) ≈ f(a) + f′(a)δx. In Leibniz notation the rule is δy ≈ (dy/dx)δx. It is the definition of the derivative read backwards, since δy/δx tends to dy/dx as δx tends to 0.","By Taylor's theorem the error is about ½f″(a)δx², so halving δx roughly quarters the error. When f″(a) < 0 the curve is concave and the tangent lies above it, so the estimate is too big. When f″(a) > 0 the curve is convex and the estimate is too small.","A-level and IB tasks include estimating roots such as √26 and powers such as 1.02⁵. Others ask for the change in area or volume, as in δA ≈ 2πr δr. A related form uses relative change. For V = (4/3)πr³ we get δV/V ≈ 3δr/r, so a 2% rise in radius gives about a 6% rise in volume.","A common mistake is to use the slope at the new point, which needs the value you are trying to find. Others are to report δy when f(a + δx) is asked for, to treat δx as the new x, and to apply the method with a large step. Students should always say which point a they used and why it is easy. The lab zooms in until the curve and tangent merge and builds √4.1 card by card. It then measures how the error scales with δx, judges over or under on three curves, and grows a circle by a thin ring."]},"keyIdea":"Near x = a a smooth curve is almost its tangent line, so for a small change δx we can use f(a + δx) ≈ f(a) + f′(a)δx, or δy ≈ (dy/dx)δx. The error shrinks like δx², and the sign of f″ tells you if the estimate is too big or too small.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the tangent estimate come out too big or too small? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⬆️ Over-estimate","⬇️ Under-estimate"],"items":[["√4.1 from the tangent at x = 4",0],["ln 1.1 from the tangent at x = 1",0],["√26 from the tangent at x = 25",0],["sin 0.1 from the tangent at x = 0",0],["e^0.1 from the tangent at x = 0",1],["2.1² from the tangent at x = 2",1],["1.02⁵ from the tangent at x = 1",1],["1/1.1 from the tangent to 1/x at x = 1",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagapprox","caption":"Flagship lab: zoom until √x meets its tangent, build √4.1 ≈ 2.025, watch the error shrink like δx², judge over or under, grow a circle.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ubY3aukn0T":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Differentiate y = 1/(x² + 1).","choices":["−2x/(x² + 1)²","1/(2x)","2x/(x² + 1)²"],"answer":0,"hint":"u = 1, so u′ = 0. Then the top is 0·v − 1·2x.","explain":"The top is −2x and the bottom is (x² + 1)². 1/(2x) is u′/v′ upside down, and 2x/(x² + 1)² has lost the minus sign."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"y = (x + {a})/(x + {b}). Find dy/dx at x = {=1-b}.","answer":"b-a","hint":"u′ = v′ = 1, so the top is (x + {b}) − (x + {a}).","explain":"The top is {b} − {a} = {=b-a}, so dy/dx = {=b-a}/(x + {b})². At x = {=1-b} the bottom is 1, so dy/dx = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[4,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A student gets d/dx [x/(x + 1)] = −1/(x + 1)². What went wrong?","choices":["They used uv′ − u′v, the wrong order","Nothing, it is correct","They forgot to square v"],"answer":0,"hint":"x/(x + 1) is rising for x > 0. What sign should the slope have?","explain":"The correct top is u′v − uv′ = (x + 1) − x = 1. Reversing the order flips the sign, giving a negative slope for a rising curve."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where does the v² in the quotient rule come from?","choices":["Differentiating v⁻¹ with the chain rule gives −v′/v²","You square the top and the bottom","It is a convention"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write u/v as u·v⁻¹, then differentiate v⁻¹ with the chain rule: bring down the power and multiply by v′.","explain":"d/dx (v⁻¹) = −1·v⁻²·v′ = −v′/v². The product rule then gives u′/v − uv′/v², and over a common denominator that is (u′v − uv′)/v²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"y = x²/(x + {a}) has a stationary point at x = 0. Find the x-coordinate of the other one.","answer":"-2*a","hint":"The top of dy/dx is 2x(x + {a}) − x² = x(x + {=2*a}).","explain":"dy/dx = x(x + {=2*a})/(x + {a})². The top is zero at x = 0 and x = −{=2*a}. The bottom is never part of the answer.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"y = x²/(x + 1). Why is x = −1 not a stationary point?","choices":["The function is undefined there: it is an asymptote","dy/dx = 0 there","It is a stationary point"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the denominator at x = −1?","explain":"At x = −1 the bottom is zero, so y is undefined and the graph has a vertical asymptote. Stationary points come from the top of dy/dx being zero."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Differentiate y = eˣ/x.","choices":["eˣ(x − 1)/x²","eˣ","eˣ(1 − x)/x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"u = eˣ and v = x, so u′ = eˣ and v′ = 1.","explain":"u′v − uv′ = x eˣ − eˣ = eˣ(x − 1), over x². eˣ is u′/v′, and eˣ(1 − x)/x² has the order reversed."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"y = (x + {a})/(x − {b}). Find dy/dx at x = {=b+1}.","answer":"-(a+b)","hint":"u′ = 1 and v′ = 1, so the top is (x − {b}) − (x + {a}).","explain":"The top is (x − {b}) − (x + {a}) = −{=a+b}, so dy/dx = −{=a+b}/(x − {b})². At x = {=b+1} the bottom is 1, so dy/dx = −{=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"y = {a}x/(x + 1). Find dy/dx at x = 0.","answer":"a","hint":"u = {a}x, v = x + 1. Use (u′v − uv′)/v².","explain":"u′v − uv′ = {a}(x + 1) − {a}x = {a}, so dy/dx = {a}/(x + 1)². At x = 0 that is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Differentiate y = sin x / x.","choices":["(x cos x − sin x)/x²","(sin x − x cos x)/x²","cos x"],"answer":0,"hint":"u = sin x, v = x. The top is u′v − uv′.","explain":"u′v − uv′ = x cos x − sin x, over x². The second choice has the order reversed and cos x is u′/v′."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is d/dx (tan x) = sec²x?","choices":["(cos²x + sin²x)/cos²x = 1/cos²x","The derivative of sin x divided by the derivative of cos x","Because tan x = sec x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write tan x = sin x / cos x and use the quotient rule.","explain":"u′v − uv′ = cos x·cos x − sin x·(−sin x) = cos²x + sin²x = 1, over cos²x. Dividing derivatives gives −cot x, which is wrong."}],"explain":{"kid":["A quotient is one function divided by another, like x/(x + 1). It is tempting to differentiate the top and the bottom and divide, u′/v′. That fails. For x/(x + 1) it gives 1 everywhere, but the true slope falls as x grows.","The quotient rule says: if y = u/v, then dy/dx = (u′v − uv′)/v². Say it as: bottom times derivative of top, minus top times derivative of bottom, all over the bottom squared. Because of the minus sign, the order matters. Swap it and every answer flips sign.","Where does it come from? Write u/v as u times v⁻¹. The chain rule gives the derivative of v⁻¹ as −v′/v². Then the product rule gives u′/v − uv′/v². Put both over v² and you get the rule.","Two big uses. First, tan x = sin x / cos x, so its derivative is (cos²x + sin²x)/cos²x = sec²x. Second, to find stationary points of a quotient, set the top of dy/dx to zero. Where the bottom is zero, the function is undefined, so that is an asymptote, not a turning point."],"adult":["In the DfE A level mathematics subject content, students must differentiate using the product rule, the quotient rule and the chain rule, and use these to find stationary points. The AP Calculus AB course covers this as Topic 2.9, The Quotient Rule, under the learning objective of calculating derivatives of products and quotients.","Students should know u′v − uv′ over v² and also see why it is true. The quickest proof writes u/v as u·v⁻¹, then uses the chain rule on v⁻¹ and the product rule on the result. This links three rules they already know.","Common mistakes: writing u′/v′; reversing the order to uv′ − u′v, which gives the right size with the wrong sign; forgetting to square v; expanding the denominator when it should stay factorised; and setting the whole derivative to zero by clearing denominators without checking where v = 0.","Encourage a quick sign check. If the original function is clearly rising, the derivative must be positive there. For tan x, the result sec²x is never below 1, which matches a graph that is always climbing."]},"keyIdea":"To differentiate a quotient y = u/v, use dy/dx = (u′v − uv′)/v². The order on the top matters because of the minus sign, and the bottom is v squared. It follows from writing u/v as u·v⁻¹ and using the product and chain rules. It gives d/dx (tan x) = sec²x, and setting the numerator of the derivative to zero finds stationary points.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each line a correct use of the quotient rule? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Correct","❌ Slip"],"items":[["d/dx [x/(x + 1)] = 1/(x + 1)²",0],["d/dx (tan x) = sec²x",0],["d/dx [1/(x² + 1)] = −2x/(x² + 1)²",0],["d/dx (eˣ/x) = eˣ(x − 1)/x²",0],["d/dx [x/(x + 1)] = 1",1],["d/dx (sin x/x) = (sin x − x cos x)/x²",1],["d/dx [(x + 1)/(x − 2)] = −3/(x − 2)",1],["d/dx (eˣ/x) = eˣ",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagquot","caption":"Flagship lab: match slope curves, assemble the rule from tiles, tune a numerator, slide along tan x and flag stationary points.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Y5xC--f9Eh":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a tangent to a curve?","choices":["A line that just touches the curve at a point, with the same steepness there","Any line that crosses the curve twice","A line through the origin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a ruler resting on the curve.","explain":"A tangent touches the curve at a point and has the curve's steepness there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Chords of y = x² near x = {a} have gradients {=2*a+1}, {=2*a+0.1}, {=2*a+0.01}, … What is the gradient at x = {a}?","answer":"2*a","hint":"What number are they closing in on?","explain":"They close in on {=2*a}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A speedometer reads 50 km/h. This is…","choices":["an instantaneous rate of change","the average over the whole trip","the distance travelled"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it about one moment, or the whole trip?","explain":"It shows the rate at that moment: an instantaneous rate."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why can't we use a gap of exactly 0 to find the rate at a point?","choices":["Change in y ÷ change in x would be 0 ÷ 0, which has no value","Because the answer is always 0","Because tangents don't exist"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the run if the gap is 0?","explain":"Both changes would be 0, and 0 ÷ 0 has no value. So we let the gap shrink instead."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"As the second point slides towards the first, the chord…","choices":["gets closer and closer to the tangent","gets steeper and steeper for ever","turns into the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture it on a curve.","explain":"The chord turns until it matches the tangent at that point."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"For y = x², what is the gradient of the chord from x = 1 to x = 1.5?","answer":2.5,"hint":"1.5² = 2.25.","explain":"(2.25 − 1) ÷ 0.5 = 1.25 ÷ 0.5 = 2.5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The tangent to y = x² at x = 0 is…","choices":["flat (gradient 0)","vertical","the line y = x"],"answer":0,"hint":"The chord gradient from 0 to h is h.","explain":"The chord gradient h shrinks to 0, so the tangent is flat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"On a distance–time graph, which line shows the speed at exactly t = 2 s?","choices":["The tangent touching the curve at t = 2","The chord from t = 0 to t = 2","The horizontal line through the point at t = 2","The line from the origin to the point at t = 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line has the same steepness as the curve right there?","explain":"The tangent matches the curve’s steepness at that instant; the others measure averages or nothing at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Average speeds from t = 2 s: over 1 s, 24.5 m/s; over 0.1 s, 20.09 m/s; over 0.01 s, 19.65 m/s. Best estimate of the speed at 2 s?","choices":["About 19.6 m/s","24.5 m/s, the first value","About 21.4 m/s, the mean of the three","0 m/s, because no time passes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at where the values are heading, not their average.","explain":"They settle towards 19.6 m/s as the gap shrinks, so that is the instantaneous speed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"For y = x², find the chord gradient from x = {a} to x = {=a+1}.","answer":"2*a+1","hint":"Rise ÷ run, and the run is 1.","explain":"{=(a+1)*(a+1)} − {=a*a} = {=2*a+1}, and the run is 1, so the gradient is {=2*a+1}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The phone has fallen 19.6 m after 2 s. A student says its speed at 2 s is 19.6 ÷ 2 = 9.8 m/s. What went wrong?","choices":["That is the average speed since it was dropped, not the speed at 2 s","Nothing: speed is distance ÷ time","They should have multiplied, 19.6 × 2","The speed at 2 s is the distance, 19.6 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"It was speeding up the whole time.","explain":"Distance ÷ total time is the average from 0 s. Because the phone speeds up, its speed at 2 s is higher: 19.6 m/s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"For y = x², the chord from x = {a} to x = {a} + h has gradient {=2*a} + h. What does this approach as h shrinks to 0?","answer":"2*a","hint":"What is {=2*a} + 0?","explain":"As h shrinks, {=2*a} + h gets closer to {=2*a}. That is the gradient of the tangent at x = {a}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"For y = x², the chord from x = {a} to x = {a} + h has gradient {=2*a} + h. Find it when h = 0.1.","answer":"2*a+0.1","hint":"Add 0.1.","explain":"{=2*a} + 0.1 = {=2*a+0.1}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"For y = x², find the average rate of change from x = 3 to x = 4.","answer":7,"hint":"(16 − 9) ÷ 1.","explain":"(16 − 9) ÷ 1 = 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"For y = x², find the average rate of change from x = 3 to x = 3.1.","answer":6.1,"hint":"3.1² = 9.61.","explain":"(9.61 − 9) ÷ 0.1 = 0.61 ÷ 0.1 = 6.1."}],"explain":{"kid":["A speedometer shows your speed right now, not your average. How do we find a rate at one moment? Take a second point very close to the first. Then find the average rate over that tiny gap.","For y = x² at x = 3: from 3 to 4 the rate is 7. From 3 to 3.1 it is 6.1. From 3 to 3.01 it is 6.01. These close in on 6. So the rate at x = 3 is 6."],"adult":["A phone falls from a tower: after t seconds it has dropped s = 4.9t² metres. Average speed over a gap is Δs ÷ Δt, the slope of the chord between two points on the graph.","Shrink the gap and the averages settle: 24.5, 20.09, 19.65, 19.605 m/s … closing in on 19.6 m/s at t = 2 s. Zoom in on a smooth curve and it looks straight; that straight line is the tangent, and its slope is the instantaneous rate."]},"familyTip":"In a car, compare the speedometer with the average speed for the whole trip.","keyIdea":"Speed at an instant is what average speed settles on as the time gap shrinks to zero: the slope of the tangent.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Shrink h and watch the chord (red) turn into the tangent (purple) at x = 3.","opts":{"mode":"secant","f":"x*x","a":3,"h":1,"xr":[0,5],"yr":[-2,22]}},"lab":{"type":"flagderiv","caption":"Flagship lab: a falling phone. Shrink the time gap, zoom in, and catch the speed at one instant.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_P9vcHUW9CJ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Find the limit of {a}x − {b} as x → {c}.","answer":"a*c-b","hint":"Substitute.","explain":"{a} × {c} − {b} = {=a*c-b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,5]},"where":"a*c>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Simplify, then find the limit as x → {a}: (x² − {=a*a}) ÷ (x − {a}).","answer":"2*a","hint":"Factorise the top as a difference of two squares.","explain":"x² − {=a*a} = (x − {a})(x + {a}), so the expression is x + {a}, which tends to {=2*a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"f(1.9) = 4.9, f(1.99) = 4.99, f(2.01) = 5.01 and f(2.1) = 5.1. What is the likely limit as x → 2?","choices":["5","4.99","5.1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look from both sides.","explain":"From below and above the outputs close in on 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which statement is true?","choices":["A limit can exist where the function has no value","A limit always equals f(a)","If f(a) is 0 ÷ 0, the limit is 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of (x² − 1) ÷ (x − 1) at x = 1.","explain":"That function has no value at 1, but its limit there is 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Find the limit of (x² − 9) ÷ (x − 3) as x → 3.","answer":6,"hint":"x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3).","explain":"It simplifies to x + 3, which tends to 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does lim (x → 4) f(x) = 10 mean?","choices":["As x gets closer to 4, f(x) gets closer to 10","f(10) = 4","f(x) is never more than 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read it aloud: the limit as x tends to 4.","explain":"As x approaches 4, the outputs approach 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Find the limit of ({a}x² + {b}x) ÷ x as x → 0.","answer":"b","hint":"Divide each term by x first.","explain":"For x ≠ 0 it is {a}x + {b}, which tends to {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the limit of 2x + 1 as x tends to 3?","answer":7,"hint":"It's a smooth expression, so substitute.","explain":"2 × 3 + 1 = 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the limit of {a}x + {b} as x tends to {c}.","answer":"a*c+b","hint":"Substitute x = {c}.","explain":"{a} × {c} + {b} = {=a*c+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"f(x) = (x² − 1) ÷ (x − 1) gives 1.9, 1.99, 1.999 at x = 0.9, 0.99, 0.999. What is the limit as x → 1?","choices":["2","1.999","There is no limit, because f(1) is 0 ÷ 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where are the outputs heading?","explain":"They close in on 2. f(1) having no value doesn't stop the limit existing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"For x ≠ {a}, (x² − {=a*a}) ÷ (x − {a}) = x + {a}. Find the limit as x → {a}.","answer":"2*a","hint":"Substitute into x + {a}.","explain":"x + {a} → {a} + {a} = {=2*a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Find the limit of x² + {b} as x tends to {c}.","answer":"c*c+b","hint":"Substitute x = {c}.","explain":"{c}² + {b} = {=c*c} + {b} = {=c*c+b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at f(x) = (x² − 1) ÷ (x − 1). At x = 1 you get 0 ÷ 0, which has no value. Now try x = 0.9, 0.99 and 0.999. You get 1.9, 1.99 and 1.999. The outputs close in on 2.","We say the limit as x tends to 1 is 2. It is about where f is heading, not what happens at the point itself."],"adult":["Often algebra shows the limit. x² − 1 = (x − 1)(x + 1), so for x ≠ 1, f(x) = x + 1. As x → 1, x + 1 → 2. We write lim (x → 1) f(x) = 2.","If f is a smooth expression that can be worked out at a, the limit is just f(a). Limits matter most when you can't substitute, as with 0 ÷ 0. That is exactly what happens when a chord shrinks to a point."]},"familyTip":"Halve a number again and again: 1, 0.5, 0.25, … What value is it heading for?","keyIdea":"A limit is the value f(x) gets closer to as x gets closer to a number, even if f can't be worked out exactly there.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"f(x) = (x² − 1) ÷ (x − 1) has a hole at x = 1. Move x closer and watch f(x).","opts":{"mode":"limit","f":"(x*x-1)/(x-1)","a":1,"start":0.5,"xr":[-1,3],"yr":[-1,5]}}},"mt_a-yA19-IoO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"f(x) = x² has derivative 2x. Find the gradient at x = −{a}.","answer":"0-2*a","hint":"Double a negative number.","explain":"2 × (−{a}) = −{=2*a}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do we simplify before letting h → 0?","choices":["To cancel h first, so we never divide by 0","Because h is always 1","To make the answer bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would h = 0 do to ÷ h?","explain":"Cancelling h first avoids dividing by zero."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Expand (x + h)².","choices":["x² + 2xh + h²","x² + h²","x² + xh + h²"],"answer":0,"hint":"(x + h)(x + h).","explain":"x² + xh + xh + h² = x² + 2xh + h². Forgetting the middle term is a common mistake."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"For f(x) = {a}x, simplify the chord gradient ({a}(x + h) − {a}x) ÷ h.","answer":"a","hint":"Expand the bracket.","explain":"{a}x + {a}h − {a}x = {a}h, and {a}h ÷ h = {a}. A straight line has the same gradient everywhere.","params":{"a":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x² has derivative {=2*a}x. Find the gradient at x = {c}.","answer":"2*a*c","hint":"Substitute x = {c}.","explain":"{=2*a} × {c} = {=2*a*c}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"c":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"lim (h → 0) of (f(x + h) − f(x)) ÷ h is the gradient of…","choices":["the tangent at x","the chord from 0 to x","the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"A chord with zero width.","explain":"As the chord shrinks it becomes the tangent at x."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"For f(x) = x³, f(x + h) = x³ + 3x²h + 3xh² + h³. What is the derivative?","choices":["3x²","3x² + 3xh + h²","x³"],"answer":0,"hint":"Subtract x³, divide by h, then let h → 0.","explain":"You get 3x² + 3xh + h², which tends to 3x² as h → 0."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In (f(x + h) − f(x)) ÷ h, what does h stand for?","choices":["A small step along the x-axis","The height of the curve","The gradient"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's the run of the chord.","explain":"h is the small step in x between the two points."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"For f(x) = x², the gradient at any point is 2x. What is the gradient at x = {a}?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Double it.","explain":"2 × {a} = {=2*a}.","params":{"a":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"For f(x) = x², (f(x + h) − f(x)) ÷ h simplifies to…","choices":["2x + h","2x","x² + h"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand (x + h)² first.","explain":"(x² + 2xh + h² − x²) ÷ h = (2xh + h²) ÷ h = 2x + h. Only then do we let h → 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"For f(x) = {a}x², the chord gradient simplifies to {=2*a}x + {a}h. What is the gradient at x = {c}?","answer":"2*a*c","hint":"Let h → 0, then substitute.","explain":"As h → 0 it becomes {=2*a}x. At x = {c}: {=2*a} × {c} = {=2*a*c}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"c":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"For f(x) = x² + {b}x, the chord gradient is 2x + {b} + h. What is the gradient at x = {c}?","answer":"2*c+b","hint":"Let h → 0 first.","explain":"As h → 0 it becomes 2x + {b}. At x = {c}: {=2*c} + {b} = {=2*c+b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["To find the gradient of a curve at a point, take a chord to a point a tiny step h further along. Its gradient is (f(x + h) − f(x)) ÷ h. Then let h shrink to 0.","For f(x) = x², f(x + h) = x² + 2xh + h². Take away x² to get 2xh + h². Divide by h to get 2x + h. As h → 0 this becomes 2x. So the gradient of y = x² at any point is 2x."],"adult":["This is called differentiating from first principles. The derivative is f′(x) = lim (h → 0) of (f(x + h) − f(x)) ÷ h.","The key step is to expand and simplify before letting h → 0, so that you never divide by zero. For f(x) = 3x²: (3(x + h)² − 3x²) ÷ h = 6x + 3h, which tends to 6x."]},"familyTip":"Draw y = x² carefully. Measure the steepness at x = 1 and x = 2, and compare with 2x.","keyIdea":"The derivative is the limit of the chord gradient (f(x + h) − f(x)) ÷ h as h tends to 0.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"First principles on y = x²: the chord gradient (f(1 + h) − f(1)) ÷ h as h → 0.","opts":{"mode":"secant","f":"x*x","a":1,"h":2,"xr":[-1,4],"yr":[-2,12]}}},"mt_h07IxxUQwx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"f′(x) = {a}x². Find f′({c}).","answer":"a*c*c","hint":"Square {c}, then multiply.","explain":"{a} × {c}² = {a} × {=c*c} = {=a*c*c}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam writes f′(3) = 3f′. What is wrong?","choices":["f′(3) means the gradient at x = 3, not 3 times anything","Nothing","It should be f(3)′"],"answer":0,"hint":"Brackets show the input.","explain":"f′(3) is the gradient function evaluated at x = 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"For y = f(x), which pair mean the same thing?","choices":["dy/dx and f′(x)","dy/dx and y ÷ x","f′(x) and f(x)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both are names for the gradient function.","explain":"dy/dx and f′(x) both mean the derivative."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"V is volume in litres and t is time in minutes. dV/dt = {r} and stays the same. How many litres flow in during 3 minutes?","answer":"3*r","hint":"Rate × time.","explain":"{r} litres per minute × 3 minutes = {=3*r} litres.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"If dy/dx = 0 at a point, the graph there is…","choices":["flat (horizontal tangent)","vertical","crossing the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"A gradient of 0.","explain":"Zero gradient means the tangent is horizontal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"If dy/dx is negative at a point, then…","choices":["y is decreasing as x increases","y is negative","x is negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"dy/dx describes the slope, not the value.","explain":"A negative gradient means the graph goes down there. y itself could be positive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"dy/dx = 3x² − {b}. Find dy/dx at x = {c}.","answer":"3*c*c-b","hint":"Square first, then multiply by 3.","explain":"3 × {=c*c} − {b} = {=3*c*c} − {b} = {=3*c*c-b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[1,4]},"where":"3*c*c>b"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How is f′(x) usually read?","choices":["f dash x (or f prime x)","f times x","f to the power x"],"answer":0,"hint":"The mark is a dash.","explain":"f′(x) is read 'f dash x' in the UK and 'f prime of x' in the US."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"f′(x) = 2x. Find f′({a}).","answer":"2*a","hint":"Put {a} into f′.","explain":"f′({a}) = 2 × {a} = {=2*a}.","params":{"a":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What does dy/dx mean?","choices":["The rate of change of y as x changes","d × y ÷ (d × x)","y divided by x"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's notation, not a fraction to cancel.","explain":"dy/dx is the rate of change of y with respect to x. You can't cancel the d's."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"dy/dx = {a}x + {b}. Find the gradient when x = {c}.","answer":"a*c+b","hint":"Substitute x = {c}.","explain":"{a} × {c} + {b} = {=a*c+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"s is distance and t is time. What is ds/dt?","choices":["Velocity: how fast distance changes","Distance × time","The total distance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rate of change of s with respect to t.","explain":"ds/dt is the rate of change of distance: the velocity."}],"explain":{"kid":["There are two common ways to write the derivative. If y = f(x), we can write f′(x), said 'f dash x' (or 'f prime x'). Or we can write dy/dx, said 'dee y by dee x'.","dy/dx is not a fraction you can cancel. It means the rate of change of y as x changes. If y = x², then dy/dx = 2x. At x = 3, dy/dx = 6."],"adult":["Leibniz's dy/dx reminds you of 'change in y over change in x'. Lagrange's f′(x) is shorter when a function has a name. Both describe the gradient function.","Other letters work too. If distance s depends on time t, then ds/dt is how fast distance changes: the velocity. f′(3) means the gradient at x = 3, not 3 times f′."]},"familyTip":"Spot rates in daily life (litres per minute, pounds per hour) and write each one as d(something)/d(something).","keyIdea":"f′(x) and dy/dx both mean the derivative: the gradient function.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag the point on y = x². dy/dx is the gradient of the tangent there.","opts":{"mode":"tangent","f":"x*x","a":1.5,"xr":[-3,3],"yr":[-2,9],"step":0.5}}},"mt_lt75XjH3E2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"f(x) = x⁴. Find f′({c}).","answer":"4*c*c*c","hint":"f′(x) = 4x³.","explain":"f′(x) = 4x³, so f′({c}) = 4 × {=c*c*c} = {=4*c*c*c}.","params":{"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"y = {a}x⁴. Find dy/dx when x = {c}.","answer":"4*a*c*c*c","hint":"dy/dx = {=4*a}x³.","explain":"dy/dx = {=4*a}x³. At x = {c}: {=4*a} × {=c*c*c} = {=4*a*c*c*c}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"c":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Differentiate x³.","choices":["3x²","3x³","x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply by 3, take one off the power.","explain":"3 × x² = 3x²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Differentiate √x.","choices":["1/(2√x)","2√x","½√x"],"answer":0,"hint":"√x is x to the power ½.","explain":"Multiply by ½ and take one off the power to get −½: ½ × x to the power −½ = 1/(2√x)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Differentiate x.","choices":["1","x","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"x is x¹.","explain":"1 × x⁰ = 1. The line y = x has gradient 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Jo differentiates x⁻³ and gets −3x⁻². What went wrong?","choices":["One less than −3 is −4, so it should be −3x⁻⁴","It should be 3x⁻²","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take one off −3.","explain":"−3 − 1 = −4, so the answer is −3x⁻⁴."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"y = {a}x². At which x is the gradient {=2*a*k}?","answer":"k","hint":"dy/dx = {=2*a}x.","explain":"{=2*a}x = {=2*a*k}, so x = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"k":[1,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Differentiate x⁵.","choices":["5x⁴","5x⁵","x⁴"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply by the power, then take one off.","explain":"5 × x⁴ = 5x⁴."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"y = {a}x³. Find dy/dx at x = {c}.","answer":"3*a*c*c","hint":"dy/dx = {=3*a}x².","explain":"dy/dx = {=3*a}x². At x = {c}: {=3*a} × {=c*c} = {=3*a*c*c}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"c":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Differentiate 7.","choices":["0","7","7x"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = 7 is a flat line.","explain":"A flat line has gradient 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"y = {a}x². Find the gradient at x = {c}.","answer":"2*a*c","hint":"dy/dx = {=2*a}x.","explain":"dy/dx = {=2*a}x, so at x = {c} it is {=2*a*c}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"c":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Differentiate 1/x.","choices":["−x⁻²","x⁻²","−x⁻¹"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write 1/x as x⁻¹ first.","explain":"1/x = x⁻¹. Multiply by −1 and take one off the power: −x⁻². A common slip is to go from −1 to 0 instead of −2."}],"explain":{"kid":["First principles always works, but it is slow. There is a short cut called the power rule. Multiply by the power, then take one off the power.","So x² becomes 2x, x³ becomes 3x², and x⁵ becomes 5x⁴. A plain x is x¹, so it becomes 1. A number on its own, like 7, has gradient 0."],"adult":["The power rule works for any power, including negative and fractional ones. Rewrite first: 1/x = x⁻¹, so its derivative is −x⁻², which is −1/x².","Likewise √x is x to the power ½, which gives ½ × x to the power −½, or 1/(2√x). That's why the laws of indices (exponents) matter here. Write each term as a power of x before you use the rule."]},"familyTip":"Make flashcards: a power of x on one side, its derivative on the other. Test each other.","keyIdea":"To differentiate xⁿ, multiply by the power and then take one off the power: xⁿ becomes nxⁿ⁻¹.","visual":{"type":"power","caption":"Change the power n. The gradient graph is always n × x to the power (n − 1).","opts":{"n":3}}},"mt_jRXV4nBsz_":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"y = {a}x³ + {b}. Find dy/dx at x = {c}.","answer":"3*a*c*c","hint":"The + {b} disappears.","explain":"dy/dx = {=3*a}x², so at x = {c} it is {=3*a*c*c}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"f(x) = x² + {b}x + {c}. Find f′({d}).","answer":"2*d+b","hint":"f′(x) = 2x + {b}.","explain":"f′(x) = 2x + {b}, so f′({d}) = {=2*d} + {b} = {=2*d+b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9],"d":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the derivative of a constant, such as 12?","choices":["0","12","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = 12 is flat.","explain":"A flat line has gradient 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Differentiate 4x³ − 2x.","choices":["12x² − 2","12x² − 2x","4x² − 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Term by term.","explain":"4 × 3x² = 12x² and −2x becomes −2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Differentiate (x + 2)².","choices":["2x + 4","2x + 2","2(x + 2)²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand first.","explain":"(x + 2)² = x² + 4x + 4, so the derivative is 2x + 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"f(x) = x² − {=2*k}x. At what x is the gradient 0?","answer":"k","hint":"f′(x) = 2x − {=2*k}.","explain":"2x − {=2*k} = 0, so x = {k}.","params":{"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"f(x) = 3x² + 5. Which is correct?","choices":["f′(x) = 6x","f′(x) = 6x + 5","f′(x) = 3x"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the 5?","explain":"3 × 2x = 6x, and the constant 5 disappears."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"y = {a}x² + {b}x + {c}. Find dy/dx at x = 1.","answer":"2*a+b","hint":"dy/dx = {=2*a}x + {b}.","explain":"dy/dx = {=2*a}x + {b}. At x = 1: {=2*a} + {b} = {=2*a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Differentiate 5x² + 3x + 4.","choices":["10x + 3","10x + 7","5x + 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the + 4?","explain":"10x + 3. The constant 4 has gradient 0, so it disappears."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"y = x³ + {b}x. Find the gradient at x = {c}.","answer":"3*c*c+b","hint":"dy/dx = 3x² + {b}.","explain":"dy/dx = 3x² + {b}. At x = {c}: {=3*c*c} + {b} = {=3*c*c+b}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"c":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Differentiate x²(x + 1).","choices":["3x² + 2x","2x","2x(x + 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand first.","explain":"x²(x + 1) = x³ + x², so the derivative is 3x² + 2x. You can't just multiply the derivatives."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"f(x) = {a}x² − {b}x. Find f′({c}).","answer":"2*a*c-b","hint":"f′(x) = {=2*a}x − {b}.","explain":"f′(x) = {=2*a}x − {b}, so f′({c}) = {=2*a*c} − {b} = {=2*a*c-b}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,5]},"where":"2*a*c>b"}],"explain":{"kid":["You can differentiate a sum one term at a time. For y = x³ + x², dy/dx = 3x² + 2x.","A number in front stays in front, so 5x² becomes 5 × 2x = 10x. A number on its own, like + 4, becomes 0. So y = 5x² + 3x + 4 gives dy/dx = 10x + 3."],"adult":["In symbols, the derivative of af + bg is af′ + bg′. This is what lets you differentiate any polynomial quickly.","Products are different. The derivative of x²(x + 1) is not 2x × 1. Expand it first to x³ + x², then differentiate to get 3x² + 2x."]},"familyTip":"Write a polynomial, swap with a friend, differentiate each other's, then check together.","keyIdea":"Differentiate term by term. A number multiplying a term stays; a number on its own disappears.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"y = x² + 2x: drag the point. dy/dx = 2x + 2, term by term.","opts":{"mode":"tangent","f":"x*x+2*x","a":1,"xr":[-4,2],"yr":[-2,8],"step":0.5}}},"mt_vqlq_sKWKm":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Find the gradient of the tangent to y = x³ at x = {a}.","answer":"3*a*a","hint":"dy/dx = 3x².","explain":"3 × {=a*a} = {=3*a*a}.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The tangent to y = x² + {b} at x = {a} is y = {=2*a}x + c. Find c.","answer":"b-a*a","hint":"The point is ({a}, {=a*a+b}).","explain":"{=a*a+b} = {=2*a*a} + c, so c = {=b-a*a}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the tangent to y = x² at (1, 1)?","choices":["y = 2x − 1","y = 2x + 1","y = x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gradient 2x = 2, then find c.","explain":"1 = 2 × 1 + c, so c = −1: y = 2x − 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A tangent has gradient {m}. What is the gradient of the normal, as a decimal?","answer":"0-1/m","hint":"−1 ÷ {m}.","explain":"−1 ÷ {m} = {=0-1/m}.","params":{"m":[1,20]},"where":"100%m==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The normal to a curve at a point is…","choices":["perpendicular to the tangent there","parallel to the tangent","always the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Normal means at right angles.","explain":"It meets the tangent at a right angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben finds the tangent to y = x² at x = 2 and writes y = 2x. What went wrong?","choices":["He used 2 as the gradient; it's 2 × 2 = 4, giving y = 4x − 4","Nothing","He forgot to square 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out dy/dx at x = 2.","explain":"The gradient is 4 and the point is (2, 4), so 4 = 8 + c and c = −4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the gradient of the tangent to y = x² − 4x at x = 3?","answer":2,"hint":"dy/dx = 2x − 4.","explain":"2 × 3 − 4 = 6 − 4 = 2."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"The tangent to y = x² at x = 3 is y = 6x + c. Find c.","answer":-9,"hint":"The point is (3, 9).","explain":"9 = 6 × 3 + c = 18 + c, so c = −9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the gradient of the tangent to y = x² at x = {a}.","answer":"2*a","hint":"dy/dx = 2x.","explain":"dy/dx = 2x, so at x = {a} the gradient is {=2*a}.","params":{"a":[1,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"The tangent to y = x² at x = {a} is y = {=2*a}x + c. Find c.","answer":"0-a*a","hint":"The point is ({a}, {=a*a}).","explain":"{=a*a} = {=2*a} × {a} + c = {=2*a*a} + c, so c = −{=a*a}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A tangent has gradient 2. What is the gradient of the normal at the same point?","choices":["−1/2","1/2","−2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1.","explain":"2 × (−1/2) = −1, so the normal's gradient is −1/2."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"To find the tangent at x = a, which two things do you need?","choices":["The gradient f′(a) and the point (a, f(a))","Only f(a)","The gradient f(a) and the point (a, f′(a))"],"answer":0,"hint":"A line needs a gradient and a point.","explain":"The gradient comes from f′(a); the point is (a, f(a))."}],"explain":{"kid":["To find the tangent to a curve at a point, you need a gradient and a point. The derivative gives the gradient. The curve gives the point.","For y = x² at x = 3, the point is (3, 9). dy/dx = 2x, so the gradient is 6. Then 9 = 6 × 3 + c gives c = −9. So the tangent is y = 6x − 9."],"adult":["Use y − y₁ = m(x − x₁), with m = f′(a) and the point (a, f(a)). Rearrange into y = mx + c if needed.","The normal is the line at right angles to the tangent at the same point. Its gradient is −1 ÷ m, because perpendicular gradients multiply to −1. For y = x² at (3, 9), the normal has gradient −1/6."]},"familyTip":"Plot y = x² on graph paper. Draw your tangent at x = 3 and check that it just touches the curve.","keyIdea":"The tangent at x = a has gradient f′(a) and passes through (a, f(a)). The normal is perpendicular to it.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Tangent (purple) and normal (green) to y = x² at a point you choose. Equal scales, so the right angle looks right.","opts":{"mode":"tangent","f":"x*x","a":1,"normal":true,"xr":[-2.5,3],"yr":[-1,4],"step":0.5,"equal":true}}},"mt_XW5d2G7v01":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Use 2 left rectangles of width 1 under y = x² + {c} from x = 0 to 2. Estimate the area.","answer":"2*c+1","hint":"The heights are f(0) and f(1).","explain":"Heights {c} and {=1+c}, so the area ≈ {c} + {=1+c} = {=2*c+1}.","params":{"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Trapezium rule, strip width 1, heights y₀ = {a}, y₁ = {b}, y₂ = {c}. Estimate the area.","answer":"(a+2*b+c)/2","hint":"½ × width × (first + 2 × middle + last).","explain":"½ × ({a} + 2 × {b} + {c}) = {=(a+2*b+c)/2}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} equal strips cover x = 0 to x = {b}. How wide is each one?","answer":"b/n","hint":"Divide the length by the number of strips.","explain":"{b} ÷ {n} = {=b/n}.","params":{"n":[2,5],"b":[1,10]},"where":"(b*100)%n==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"For y = x² from 0 to 2, left rectangles give an estimate that is…","choices":["too small","too big","exact"],"answer":0,"hint":"The curve rises, so where is each rectangle's height measured?","explain":"The curve rises, so the left edge is the lowest point of each strip: every rectangle sits under the curve."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"For the same number of strips, which estimate usually follows a smooth curve most closely?","choices":["Trapezia","Left rectangles","One big rectangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shape uses both ends of each strip?","explain":"Trapezia join the curve at both ends of each strip, so they hug it better."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does doubling the number of strips do to each strip's width?","choices":["Halves it","Doubles it","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"The total length stays the same.","explain":"The same length shared by twice as many strips makes each one half as wide."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Right rectangles of width 1 under y = 2x from 0 to {n} have heights 2, 4, … up to {=2*n}. Add them up.","answer":"n*(n+1)","hint":"Add the even numbers from 2 to the last height.","explain":"2 + 4 + … + {=2*n} = {=n*(n+1)}.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Strips from x = 0 to x = {b}, with {n} equal strips. How wide is each strip?","answer":"b/n","hint":"Width = total length ÷ number of strips.","explain":"Width = {b} ÷ {n} = {=b/n}.","params":{"b":[2,8],"n":[2,5]},"where":"(b*100)%n==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Two rectangles under a curve each have width 1. Their heights are {a} and {b}. Estimate the area.","answer":"a+b","hint":"Area of each rectangle is width × height.","explain":"1 × {a} + 1 × {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What usually happens to the estimate as you use more, thinner strips?","choices":["It gets closer to the true area","It gets further away","It stays exactly the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thinner strips hug the curve.","explain":"Thinner strips leave less gap or overlap, so the estimate gets closer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"One trapezium strip has width {h} and edge heights {a} and {b}. What is its area?","answer":"h*(a+b)/2","hint":"Area of a trapezium = width × (sum of the parallel sides) ÷ 2.","explain":"{h} × ({a} + {b}) ÷ 2 = {=h*(a+b)/2}.","params":{"h":[1,4],"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["To find the area under a curve, cut it into thin strips. Each strip is close to a rectangle, and a rectangle's area is easy to find.","Add up all the strips to get an estimate. More strips that are thinner give a better estimate. A trapezium strip follows the curve more closely than a rectangle does."],"adult":["With n strips of width h = (b − a)/n, a left-rectangle estimate is h × (f(x₀) + f(x₁) + … + f(xₙ₋₁)). The trapezium rule uses both ends of each strip: h/2 × (y₀ + 2y₁ + … + 2yₙ₋₁ + yₙ).","For a curve that bends upwards, like y = x², trapezia overestimate. The error shrinks about four times each time you double n."]},"familyTip":"Draw a curve on squared paper and count squares under it. Then try strips of two squares wide, then one. Which count is closest?","keyIdea":"Cut the area into thin strips, add up the strip areas, and use more strips for a better estimate.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Change the number of strips and the method. Watch the estimate close in on the exact area under y = x².","opts":{"mode":"riemann","f":"x*x","a":0,"b":2,"n":4,"xr":[-0.2,2.4],"yr":[-0.5,5]}}},"mt_cryXWeeFPc":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"∫ 5 dx = ?","choices":["5x + C","0","5 + C"],"answer":0,"hint":"5 is 5x⁰.","explain":"5x⁰ → 5x¹ ÷ 1 = 5x, plus C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"∫ x dx = ?","choices":["x²/2 + C","1 + C","x² + C"],"answer":0,"hint":"x is x¹.","explain":"x¹ → x², then ÷ 2: x²/2 + C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"∫ {=4*a}x³ dx = Ax⁴ + C. Find A.","answer":"a","hint":"Add one to the power (4), then divide by 4.","explain":"{=4*a} ÷ 4 = {a}, so A = {a}.","params":{"a":[1,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"F(x) = x³ + {c}x is an antiderivative of f(x). Find f(2).","answer":"12+c","hint":"f is the derivative of F.","explain":"f(x) = 3x² + {c}, so f(2) = 12 + {c} = {=12+c}.","params":{"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is an antiderivative of 2x?","choices":["x² + 7","2","2x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Differentiate each choice.","explain":"x² + 7 differentiates to 2x."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"∫ x⁻² dx = ?","choices":["−x⁻¹ + C","x⁻¹ + C","−2x⁻³ + C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add one to −2, then divide by the new power.","explain":"x⁻² → x⁻¹, then ÷ (−1): −x⁻¹ + C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"What number goes in front of x² in ∫ ({=2*a}x + {b}) dx?","answer":"a","hint":"Integrate the x term only.","explain":"{=2*a}x integrates to {a}x², so the number is {a}.","params":{"a":[1,7],"b":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"∫ x³ dx = ?","choices":["x⁴/4 + C","3x² + C","x⁴ + C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add one to the power, then divide by the new power.","explain":"x³ → x⁴, then ÷ 4: x⁴/4 + C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"∫ {=3*m}x² dx = Ax³ + C. Find A.","answer":"m","hint":"Add one to the power (3), then divide by 3.","explain":"{=3*m} ÷ 3 = {m}, so A = {m}.","params":{"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"∫ {=2*a}x dx = Ax² + C. Find A.","answer":"a","hint":"x becomes x², then divide by 2.","explain":"{=2*a} ÷ 2 = {a}, so A = {a}.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why do we add + C?","choices":["A constant differentiates to 0, so it could have been there","C stands for 'curve'","To make the answer bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the derivative of 5?","explain":"Any constant has derivative 0, so we cannot tell which constant was there: we write + C."}],"explain":{"kid":["Differentiating takes a curve and gives its gradient. An antiderivative goes the other way: you know the gradient, and you find the curve.","For a power of x, add one to the power, then divide by the new power. So x² becomes x³ ÷ 3. Always add + C at the end, because a number on its own would vanish when you differentiate."],"adult":["∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C for every n except −1. Work term by term and keep any number in front: ∫6x² dx = 2x³ + C.","Check an answer by differentiating it: you should get back what you started with. The + C stands for a whole family of curves, each one a vertical shift of the others."]},"familyTip":"Play 'reverse it': one person says a derivative like 6x², the other gives a curve that has it, then both check by differentiating.","keyIdea":"Integration reverses differentiation: add one to the power, divide by the new power, and add + C.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Every curve y = x² + C has gradient 2x. Slide C: the curve moves, but its gradient at x = 1 never changes.","opts":{"mode":"family","F":"x*x","label":"x²","at":1,"xr":[-3,3],"yr":[-5,9]}}},"mt_gbnMvpN_Lj":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"y = x² + C passes through (3, {y}). Find C.","answer":"y-9","hint":"3² = 9.","explain":"{y} = 9 + C, so C = {=y-9}.","params":{"y":[10,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"dy/dx = 4x³, and y = {y} when x = 1. Find y when x = 2.","answer":"y+15","hint":"Integrate to y = x⁴ + C, find C, then put x = 2.","explain":"C = {y} − 1 = {=y-1}, so y(2) = 16 + {=y-1} = {=y+15}.","params":{"y":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why can't you find C from dy/dx alone?","choices":["Every vertical shift has the same gradient","C is always 0","dy/dx is never known"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the shifted curves have different slopes?","explain":"Moving a curve up or down doesn't change its gradient, so dy/dx can't tell them apart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A position is s = 5t² + C metres, and s = {h} when t = 0. Find C.","answer":"h","hint":"Put t = 0 in.","explain":"{h} = 0 + C, so C = {h}.","params":{"h":[1,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"y = 2x + C passes through (1, 5). What is C?","choices":["3","5","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"5 = 2 × 1 + C.","explain":"5 = 2 + C, so C = 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"y = x³ + C passes through (2, {y}). Find C.","answer":"y-8","hint":"2³ = 8.","explain":"{y} = 8 + C, so C = {=y-8}.","params":{"y":[9,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is enough to fix C?","choices":["Any one point on the curve","The gradient at one point","Nothing: C is always 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"One equation, one unknown.","explain":"One known point gives one equation, which is enough to find C."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"dy/dx = 2x, and the curve passes through (0, {c}). Find C in y = x² + C.","answer":"c","hint":"Put x = 0 and y = {c} into y = x² + C.","explain":"{c} = 0 + C, so C = {c}.","params":{"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"y = x² + C passes through (2, {y}). Find C.","answer":"y-4","hint":"Put x = 2 in: 2² = 4.","explain":"{y} = 4 + C, so C = {=y-4}.","params":{"y":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How are the curves y = x² + 1 and y = x² + 5 related?","choices":["Same shape; one is 4 units higher","They cross at x = 1","One is steeper"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare them at the same x.","explain":"At every x, the second is 4 more: the same curve moved up 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"dy/dx = 3x², and y = {c} when x = 1. Find C in y = x³ + C.","answer":"c-1","hint":"Put x = 1 in: 1³ = 1.","explain":"{c} = 1 + C, so C = {=c-1}.","params":{"c":[2,12]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you integrate, you get a family of curves. They all have the same shape, just moved up or down. That is the + C.","If you know one point on the curve, put its x and y into your answer. Then you can work out C and pick the right curve."],"adult":["Given dy/dx = f(x) and a point (x₀, y₀), integrate to get y = F(x) + C, then substitute: C = y₀ − F(x₀).","In science this point is often an initial condition, such as a starting position at t = 0. With t = 0 many terms vanish, so C is simply the starting value."]},"familyTip":"Trace one curve, then slide the tracing paper straight up. Every copy has the same slopes. Which copy goes through a dot you drew?","keyIdea":"+ C means a whole family of curves; one known point picks out the curve you want.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Slide C until the curve y = x² + C passes through the red point (1, 4).","opts":{"mode":"family","F":"x*x","label":"x²","at":1,"point":[1,4],"c":0,"xr":[-3,3],"yr":[-5,9]}}},"mt_P-VlHqNpca":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"∫ from {a} to {b} of 3 dx = ?","answer":"3*(b-a)","hint":"It is a rectangle of height 3.","explain":"Width {=b-a}, height 3: {=3*(b-a)}.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"If ∫ from 0 to 2 of f(x) dx = 5, what is ∫ from 2 to 0 of f(x) dx?","choices":["−5","5","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Swapping the limits…","explain":"Swapping the limits changes the sign: −5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of 2x dx is a triangle. What is its area?","answer":"b*b","hint":"The height at x = {b} is {=2*b}.","explain":"½ × {b} × {=2*b} = {=b*b}.","params":{"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"y = −1 from x = 0 to 3. What is ∫ from 0 to 3 of (−1) dx?","choices":["−3","3","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"The rectangle is below the axis.","explain":"Area 3, below the axis, so the integral is −3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What does dx stand for in ∫ f(x) dx?","choices":["The width of a strip, shrinking to zero","A second unknown to solve for","The derivative"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Σ f(x) Δx.","explain":"It is the strip width Δx in the limit."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {a} of f dx = {p} and ∫ from {a} to 10 of f dx = {q}. Find ∫ from 0 to 10 of f dx.","answer":"p+q","hint":"The two pieces join up.","explain":"{p} + {q} = {=p+q}.","params":{"a":[2,8],"p":[1,9],"q":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"As the number of strips grows, the sum of strip areas…","choices":["settles on the definite integral","grows without limit","becomes zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"The strips get thinner as well as more numerous.","explain":"The sum approaches a limit: that limit is the definite integral."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In ∫ from 1 to 4 of f(x) dx, what are 1 and 4?","choices":["The limits: where the area starts and stops","Two values of f","The number of strips"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at where they are written.","explain":"They are the limits of integration: the area runs from x = 1 to x = 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of {k} dx is the area of a rectangle. What is it?","answer":"b*k","hint":"Width × height.","explain":"Width {b}, height {k}: {b} × {k} = {=b*k}.","params":{"b":[1,9],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of x dx is the area of a triangle. What is it?","answer":"b*b/2","hint":"½ × base × height; the height is also {b}.","explain":"½ × {b} × {b} = {=b*b/2}.","params":{"b":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"∫ from −2 to 2 of x dx = ?","choices":["0","4","−4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sketch y = x: what is below the axis?","explain":"The triangle below the axis cancels the one above, so the answer is 0."}],"explain":{"kid":["The long S sign ∫ means 'sum'. The numbers at the bottom and top tell you where to start and stop.","Picture thin strips from the start to the stop. Add up their areas. As the strips get thinner, the total settles on one number. That number is the definite integral. Area below the x-axis counts as minus."],"adult":["∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = lim (n → ∞) of Σ f(xᵢ) Δx, where Δx = (b − a)/n. The dx is the strip width, shrinking to zero.","Areas below the axis count as negative, so ∫ from −1 to 1 of x dx = 0, even though the shaded region is not empty. Swapping the limits changes the sign."]},"familyTip":"Draw y = 3 from x = 0 to 4 and shade under it. Explain why ∫ from 0 to 4 of 3 dx must be 12 before doing any algebra.","keyIdea":"∫ from a to b of f(x) dx is what the sum of strip areas settles on as the strips get thinner: the signed area.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Drag the two limits on y = 2 − x. Area above the x-axis counts as positive, area below as negative.","opts":{"mode":"definite","f":"2-x","a":0,"b":3,"xr":[-1,4],"yr":[-2.5,3.5]}},"lab":{"type":"flagarea","caption":"Flagship lab: cruise control. Distance is the area under the speed graph; then strips, negative area and limits.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ckLHNZCBUi":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of 3x² dx = ?","answer":"b*b*b","hint":"An antiderivative of 3x² is x³.","explain":"[x³] from 0 to {b} = {=b*b*b}.","params":{"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"∫ from 1 to {b} of 2x dx = ?","answer":"b*b-1","hint":"An antiderivative of 2x is x².","explain":"{=b*b} − 1 = {=b*b-1}.","params":{"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which pair of processes undo each other?","choices":["Differentiating and integrating","Adding and integrating","Squaring and differentiating"],"answer":0,"hint":"That is the theorem.","explain":"Differentiation and integration are inverse processes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A(x) = ∫ from 0 to x of (t + {c}) dt. Find A′(4).","answer":"4+c","hint":"A′(x) is the curve's height at x.","explain":"A′(4) = 4 + {c} = {=4+c}.","params":{"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"If F′(x) = f(x), which equals ∫ from a to b of f(x) dx?","choices":["F(b) − F(a)","F(a) − F(b)","f(b) − f(a)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Top limit first.","explain":"F(b) − F(a)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"∫ from 0 to 2 of {=4*k}x³ dx = ?","answer":"16*k","hint":"An antiderivative is {k}x⁴.","explain":"{k} × 2⁴ − 0 = {=16*k}.","params":{"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The area function A(x) grows fastest where…","choices":["f(x) is largest","f(x) is zero","x is zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"A′(x) = f(x).","explain":"Its gradient is f(x), so it rises fastest where f is biggest."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A(x) is the area under f from 0 to x. What is A′(x)?","choices":["f(x)","A(x)²","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"How fast does the area grow?","explain":"The area grows at the rate of the curve's height: A′(x) = f(x)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"F(x) = x² has F′(x) = 2x. Find ∫ from {a} to {b} of 2x dx.","answer":"b*b-a*a","hint":"Work out F(b) − F(a).","explain":"F({b}) − F({a}) = {=b*b} − {=a*a} = {=b*b-a*a}.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"The height of f at x = {x} is {h}. How fast is the area A(x) growing there?","answer":"h","hint":"A′(x) = f(x).","explain":"A′({x}) = f({x}) = {h}.","params":{"x":[1,9],"h":[1,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why does + C not matter in F(b) − F(a)?","choices":["It cancels when you subtract","C is always zero","You add it twice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write (F(b) + C) − (F(a) + C).","explain":"The two Cs cancel, leaving F(b) − F(a)."}],"explain":{"kid":["Imagine painting the area under a curve, moving to the right. How fast does the painted area grow? As fast as the curve is tall.","So the area has gradient f. That means the area is an antiderivative of f. To find an area from a to b, find an antiderivative F and work out F(b) − F(a)."],"adult":["If A(x) = ∫ from a to x of f(t) dt, then A′(x) = f(x): a thin extra strip has area about f(x) × h, so (A(x + h) − A(x))/h → f(x).","Any two antiderivatives differ by a constant, so F(b) − F(a) is the same whichever F you pick; the + C cancels. Differentiation and integration are inverse processes."]},"familyTip":"Fill a tall thin glass and a wide bowl from the same tap. Which water level rises faster? Link the width to how fast the 'area' grows.","keyIdea":"The rate the area grows is the height of the curve, so integrating and differentiating undo each other: ∫ₐᵇ f dx = F(b) − F(a).","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Drag the right edge. The area graph (right) rises at a rate equal to the height of f (left).","opts":{"mode":"ftc","f":"x/2+1","a":0,"start":2,"xr":[0,4],"yr":[0,4],"ar":[0,9],"step":0.25}}},"mt_KceIV8SGuG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of 4x³ dx = ?","answer":"b*b*b*b","hint":"[x⁴] from 0 to {b}.","explain":"{b}⁴ = {=b*b*b*b}.","params":{"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"∫ from {a} to {b} of 6x dx = ?","answer":"3*(b*b-a*a)","hint":"[3x²] from {a} to {b}.","explain":"3 × {=b*b} − 3 × {=a*a} = {=3*(b*b-a*a)}.","params":{"a":[0,3],"b":[4,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of {k} dx = ?","answer":"k*b","hint":"[{k}x] from 0 to {b}.","explain":"{k} × {b} = {=k*b}.","params":{"k":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"∫ from 0 to 2 of (x² − 2x) dx is negative. Why?","choices":["The curve is below the x-axis between 0 and 2","Integrals are always negative","The limits are backwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try x = 1.","explain":"At x = 1 the curve is at −1: the whole piece from 0 to 2 lies below the axis."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Do you need + C in a definite integral?","choices":["No, it cancels","Yes, always","Only if the answer is negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"(F(b) + C) − (F(a) + C).","explain":"It cancels, so leave it out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"∫ from 1 to {b} of 2x dx = ?","answer":"b*b-1","hint":"[x²] from 1 to {b}.","explain":"{=b*b} − 1 = {=b*b-1}.","params":{"b":[2,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"∫ from 0 to 1 of ({=3*a}x² + {=2*b}x) dx = ?","answer":"a+b","hint":"[{a}x³ + {b}x²] from 0 to 1.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of 2x dx = ?","answer":"b*b","hint":"[x²] from 0 to {b}.","explain":"{=b*b} − 0 = {=b*b}.","params":{"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"∫ from 1 to {b} of 3x² dx = ?","answer":"b*b*b-1","hint":"[x³] from 1 to {b}.","explain":"{=b*b*b} − 1 = {=b*b*b-1}.","params":{"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"[x²] from 1 to 3 means…","choices":["3² − 1²","1² − 3²","3² + 1²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Top limit first, then subtract.","explain":"F(3) − F(1) = 9 − 1 = 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"∫ from 0 to {b} of (2x + {c}) dx = ?","answer":"b*b+c*b","hint":"[x² + {c}x] from 0 to {b}.","explain":"{=b*b} + {=c*b} = {=b*b+c*b}.","params":{"b":[1,6],"c":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["There are three steps. First, integrate each term. Second, put the answer in square brackets with the limits at the side. Third, put in the top number, then the bottom number, and take them away.","If part of the graph is below the x-axis, that part counts as minus. To find a total area, split the integral where the graph crosses the axis."],"adult":["∫ from 1 to 3 of x² dx = [x³/3] from 1 to 3 = 9 − 1/3 = 26/3. Leave out + C; it would cancel.","A negative answer means more signed area lies below the axis than above. For the geometric area, find the roots, integrate each piece separately, and add the sizes."]},"familyTip":"Check an answer with a sketch: is the region mostly above the axis (positive) or below (negative)? Is the size sensible?","keyIdea":"Integrate, write [F(x)] with the limits, then work out F(top) − F(bottom); the result is signed area.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Drag the limits on y = x² − 2x. The integral is F(b) − F(a) with F = x³/3 − x², and area below counts as negative.","opts":{"mode":"definite","f":"x*x-2*x","a":0,"b":3,"xr":[-1,4],"yr":[-2,5]}}},"mt_g7J4GJuLrf":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"v = 3t² m/s, and s = {s} m at t = 0. Find s, in metres, at t = 2.","answer":"8+s","hint":"Integrate: s = t³ + C.","explain":"s = t³ + {s}, so s(2) = 8 + {s} = {=8+s} m.","params":{"s":[0,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Displacement is 0 m but distance is 10 m. What happened?","choices":["It went 5 m out and 5 m back","It never moved","It moved 10 m forwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"Back where it started.","explain":"It ended at the start (displacement 0) after travelling 10 m in total."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Walk forwards at 2 m/s for {a} s, then backwards at 2 m/s for {b} s. What is the displacement, in metres?","answer":"2*a-2*b","hint":"Forwards area minus backwards area.","explain":"{=2*a} − {=2*b} = {=2*a-2*b} m.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,5]},"where":"a>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Walk forwards at 2 m/s for {a} s, then backwards at 2 m/s for {b} s. What is the total distance, in metres?","answer":"2*a+2*b","hint":"Add both parts.","explain":"{=2*a} + {=2*b} = {=2*a+2*b} m.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How do you get velocity from acceleration?","choices":["Integrate a with respect to t","Differentiate a","Multiply a by s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reverse of v → a.","explain":"a = dv/dt, so v = ∫ a dt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"v = {u} + {=2*a}t m/s. Find the displacement, in metres, from t = 0 to t = 2.","answer":"2*u+4*a","hint":"s = {u}t + {a}t².","explain":"At t = 2: {=2*u} + {=4*a} = {=2*u+4*a} m.","params":{"u":[1,9],"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What fixes the + C when you integrate velocity?","choices":["Knowing the position at one time","Knowing the speed limit","Nothing; it is always zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"An initial condition.","explain":"A known position, such as s at t = 0, fixes C."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A car moves at a steady {v} m/s for {t} s. How far does it go, in metres?","answer":"v*t","hint":"Area of a rectangle on the velocity–time graph.","explain":"{v} × {t} = {=v*t} m.","params":{"v":[2,20],"t":[2,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"v = {=2*k}t m/s, starting at s = 0. Find s, in metres, at t = 3.","answer":"9*k","hint":"Integrate: s = {k}t² + C, and C = 0.","explain":"s = {k}t², so at t = 3, s = {k} × 9 = {=9*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The velocity–time graph dips below the axis. What is happening?","choices":["The object is moving backwards","It has stopped for good","It is speeding up forwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"Negative velocity means…","explain":"Negative velocity means moving in the opposite direction."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Acceleration is {a} m/s², starting from rest. Find v, in m/s, at t = {t}.","answer":"a*t","hint":"Integrate a: v = {a}t + C, and C = 0 from rest.","explain":"v = {a} × {t} = {=a*t} m/s.","params":{"a":[1,9],"t":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["On a speed–time graph, the area under the line is how far you went. Twice as fast for the same time gives twice the area.","If the velocity drops below zero, you are moving backwards. That area counts as minus for displacement, which is how far you are from the start. Total distance adds up every bit, forwards and backwards."],"adult":["s = ∫ v dt and v = ∫ a dt. Each integration adds a constant, fixed by initial conditions such as s = 0 and v = u when t = 0.","Displacement from t₁ to t₂ is ∫ v dt. Distance travelled is ∫ |v| dt: split at the times where v = 0 and add the size of each part."]},"familyTip":"Walk 5 steps forward and 3 back. Ask: how far did you walk, and how far are you from the start? Those are distance and displacement.","keyIdea":"Displacement is the integral of velocity, and velocity is the integral of acceleration; starting values fix the constants.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Velocity v = 4 − t. Drag the end time: area above the axis is moving forwards, area below is moving back.","opts":{"mode":"velocity","f":"4-x","a":0,"b":6,"xr":[0,6.5],"yr":[-3,5],"ux":"m","tu":"s"}}},"mt_Rud-oI2XyR":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A rate of {r} °C per hour lasts {t} hours. What is the net change in temperature, in °C?","answer":"r*t","hint":"Rate × time.","explain":"{r} × {t} = {=r*t} °C.","params":{"r":[1,5],"t":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A bank account gains £{a} and loses £{b} over a month. What is the net change, in £?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Gains minus losses.","explain":"{a} − {b} = £{=a-b}.","params":{"a":[100,300],"b":[10,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Fill at {r} L/min for {t} min, then drain at {d} L/min for {s} min. What is the net change, in litres?","answer":"r*t-d*s","hint":"Area above minus area below.","explain":"{=r*t} − {=d*s} = {=r*t-d*s} litres.","params":{"r":[4,9],"t":[5,10],"d":[1,3],"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What does ∫ₐᵇ r(t) dt give when r is a rate of change?","choices":["The net change from a to b","The rate at time b","The largest value of r"],"answer":0,"hint":"Signed area.","explain":"It adds up rate × time: the net change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A rate graph is above the axis, then below by the same area. The net change is…","choices":["0","double the first area","negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"They cancel.","explain":"Equal areas above and below cancel out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Fill at {r} L/min for {t} min, then drain at {d} L/min for {s} min. How much water moved in total, in litres?","answer":"r*t+d*s","hint":"Add the sizes of both parts.","explain":"{=r*t} + {=d*s} = {=r*t+d*s} litres.","params":{"r":[4,9],"t":[5,10],"d":[1,3],"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is a rate?","choices":["Litres per minute","Litres","Minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Per' means for each.","explain":"A rate compares a change with time."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Water flows into a tank at {r} litres per minute for {t} minutes. How much does the amount change, in litres?","answer":"r*t","hint":"Rate × time is the area of a rectangle.","explain":"{r} × {t} = {=r*t} litres.","params":{"r":[2,9],"t":[3,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In {a} L, then out {b} L. What is the net change in litres?","answer":"a-b","hint":"In counts as plus, out as minus.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} litres.","params":{"a":[20,60],"b":[5,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The rate graph dips below the axis. What does that part mean?","choices":["The amount is decreasing","The amount is zero","The rate is speeding up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Negative rate.","explain":"A negative rate means the amount is going down."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"50 people enter a hall and 50 leave. What is the net change?","choices":["0","100","50"],"answer":0,"hint":"In minus out.","explain":"50 − 50 = 0, even though 100 people moved."}],"explain":{"kid":["A rate tells you how fast something changes, like litres per minute flowing into a tank.","The area under a rate graph is how much the amount changed. When the rate is below zero, the amount is going down, so that area counts as minus."],"adult":["If r(t) is a rate of change, the net change from t = a to t = b is ∫ₐᵇ r(t) dt: the signed area. This works for water in a tank, money in an account or people in a room.","Net change is not total activity: 50 people in and 50 people out is a net change of 0. Add the sizes of the parts to find the total flow."]},"familyTip":"Track a piggy bank for a week: money in is positive, money spent is negative. Is the net change the same as all the money that moved?","keyIdea":"The signed area under any rate graph is the net change in the quantity; area below the axis counts as a decrease.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Water flows at r = 6 − 2t litres per minute. Drag the limits: area above adds water, area below drains it.","opts":{"mode":"definite","f":"6-2*x","a":0,"b":4,"xr":[-0.5,5],"yr":[-4,7]}}}});
